# Text source for the Control panel part of the manual. Plain text only (the build script escapes it).
# Each setting: (label exactly as on screen, what it does, example with numbers, raise or turn on, lower or turn off, cautious first week, kind)
# kind: 'num' (raise/lower), 'flag' (turn on/off), 'pick' (two or three choices; up/down hold the effect of the choices)
# Numbers come from the fictional demonstration data (as of Saturday 24 October 2026) unless an example says otherwise.

GROUPS = []

GROUPS.append(dict(
    id='cp-basic', title='Basic', shot='22-controlpanel-basic.jpg',
    shot_alt='The Basic section of the Control panel: Mode, the two exposure limits, the extension settings, the trial credit settings and the business identity checkbox, each with its hint text.',
    shot_caption='The Basic section as it appears on screen. Every label in the table below is copied from here.',
    intro='These are the rules every request meets first. Most owners never need to go beyond this section. A request is decided by the agent only when it is inside every rule here and every rule in the sections that follow.',
    items=[
        ('Mode',
         'Decides whether the agent may decide anything at all. Advisory means the agent only recommends and every request comes to you. Delegated means the agent decides the requests that are inside every rule and sends you the rest.',
         'Bayview Gelato Co asks to move invoice DEMO-301 (480 unpaid) from 18 October to 10 November. In Delegated mode the agent approves it. In Advisory mode the same request waits for you, with the assistant\'s recommendation to approve.',
         'Choose Delegated: the agent starts deciding what is inside the rules.',
         'Choose Advisory: nothing is decided by the agent, which is the safest setting. The Pause button does the same for a while and is quicker to undo.',
         'Suggestion: start with Advisory on the first day to watch what would have been approved, then move to Delegated with small limits.',
         'pick'),
        ('Most money one request may involve (the exposure of one decision)',
         'The money riding on this one request: what is still unpaid on the invoice for a payment extension, or the order less the deposit received for trial credit. A request above this limit comes to you. See "Exposure, explained" for the full picture.',
         'Limit 1,000. Bayview asks for more time on DEMO-301 with 480 unpaid: inside, the agent may approve. Harbourline Catering asks for trial credit on an order of 5,000 with a 1,000 deposit: 4,000 is at stake, so it comes to you.',
         'The agent may approve bigger single requests. More money is at risk on any one decision.',
         'More requests come to you. A limit of 0 sends every request to you.',
         'Suggestion: set it near the size of a typical invoice. Quick setup has a button that fills in "A typical open invoice". In the demonstration the value is 1,000.00.',
         'num'),
        ("Most one customer may owe you in total (the customer's total exposure)",
         'Everything one customer would still owe you across all their invoices if the request is granted. It stops many small requests from adding up for the same customer. A request that would push the total over this limit comes to you.',
         'Limit 1,500. A customer already owes 1,200 on other invoices and asks for more time on an invoice with 480 unpaid. The total is 1,680, so it comes to you, although 480 on its own is small.',
         'The agent tolerates bigger debts from one customer.',
         'Customers who already owe a lot come to you sooner.',
         'Suggestion: one and a half times the first limit, as in the demonstration (1,000.00 and 1,500.00). It should not be lower than the first limit.',
         'num'),
        ('The agent may decide payment extensions',
         'A payment extension is a customer asking for more time to pay an invoice. This switch lets the agent decide them. Off means every extension request comes to you, whatever the other rules say.',
         'On: Bayview\'s request to move DEMO-301 to 10 November is decided by the agent. Off: it waits for you with the reason "this kind of request is turned off in the policy".',
         'Turn it on to let the agent decide extensions inside the limits.',
         'Turn it off to keep all extensions for yourself.',
         'Default: off in a new workspace. Suggestion: turn it on only after you have set the limits above and below.',
         'flag'),
        ("Longest single extension (days after the invoice's current due date)",
         'How much later than the date the invoice is due now the new payment date may be. It is counted from the current due date. The date today and the invoice date play no part. A longer request comes to you.',
         'Limit 30. Invoice DEMO-301 is due 18 October. Moving it to 10 November is 23 days: inside. Moving it to 31 January 2027 is 105 days: it comes to you.',
         'The agent may give customers longer.',
         'Only short extensions are decided by the agent.',
         'Suggestion: 14 or 30 days. The demonstration uses 30.',
         'num'),
        ('Latest new payment date (days after the day the request is received)',
         'An optional second way to limit the extension: the new payment date may be at most this many days after the day the customer asks. It matters when an invoice is already late, because the first limit counts from the old due date. Empty means this limit is not used.',
         'Limit 14. A request arrives on 24 October. The new date may be 7 November at the latest. Bayview asks for 10 November, which is 17 days after the request, so it comes to you.',
         'Customers who are already late can be given more time by the agent.',
         'The agent hands over more requests. Empty switches the check off.',
         'Default: empty (not used). Suggestion: leave empty at first.',
         'num'),
        ('Most an invoice may be late in total (days after its original due date)',
         'An optional limit counted from the first due date the invoice ever had, so repeated extensions cannot go on forever. A request that would take the invoice past it comes to you. Empty means not used.',
         'Limit 60. DEMO-301 was first due 18 October, so the latest date the agent may agree to is 17 December. Two 30-day extensions fit. A third comes to you.',
         'The agent may let one invoice drift later in total.',
         'The agent stops sooner on invoices that have already been extended. Empty switches the check off.',
         'Default: empty (not used). Suggestion: 60 or 90 days once extensions are on.',
         'num'),
        ('Trial credit for new customers',
         'Trial credit is a first order on credit for a customer with no earlier invoices. This choice does not give credit to every new customer. It only lets the agent say yes when one asks and the limits below are met. Off means you decide every first credit.',
         'On, within the limits below: Northside Cafe asks for 800 of stock on 14-day terms with 200 already paid, which leaves 600 at stake. The limit is 600, so the agent approves. Off: the same request waits for you.',
         'Choose "On, within the limits below": the agent may approve small first orders.',
         'Choose "Off: the Owner decides every first credit".',
         'Default: off. Suggestion: leave it off for the first week unless the business sells to many new customers.',
         'pick'),
        ('Most trial credit exposure (the order less the deposit received)',
         'The third limit. It is what a new customer would owe you after their deposit. A trial credit above it comes to you. A bigger deposit brings a larger order inside the limit.',
         'Limit 500. An order of 600 with a deposit of 150 received leaves 450: inside. The same order with no deposit leaves 600: it comes to you. A deposit of 300 would leave 300.',
         'The agent may approve larger first orders.',
         'Smaller first orders only. Customers must pay more up front.',
         'Suggestion: the amount you would be comfortable losing on a customer you have never traded with. The demonstration uses 600.00.',
         'num'),
        ('Smallest deposit (% of the order; 0 = none asked)',
         'The part of the order a new customer must have paid up front before the agent may approve. The deposit counts only when Staff confirmed it (or Nucleon Records holds the payment).',
         'Limit 25 on an order of 600: the deposit must be at least 150. With 0 no deposit is asked and the order is judged only on the exposure limit.',
         'Customers must pay more up front. Fewer first orders are approved by the agent.',
         'Customers can order with less paid up front. Remember that the exposure limit still applies.',
         'Suggestion: 25, as in the demonstration. When you turn trial credit on, the page fills in 0 for you.',
         'num'),
        ('A new customer must confirm their business identity (ABN or ACN) before a trial credit',
         'On: before the agent may approve a trial credit, Staff give the registration number and trading name and say where they saw them. They may also give the date the business was registered. An 11-digit number is checked for typing mistakes and you are shown the result and how long the business has been registered. No rule uses either.',
         'Staff type ABN 51 824 753 556 and a registered since date of 12 March 2018. You then read "the number passes the ABN check" and "8 years 7 months". A number with one digit changed fails the check, you see that, and the outcome does not change.',
         'Turn it on: the assistant asks for identity on every trial credit.',
         'Turn it off: the assistant does not ask. The page says this is for trying things, not for real money.',
         'Default: on. Suggestion: keep it on.',
         'flag'),
    ]))

GROUPS.append(dict(
    id='cp-review', title='Approvals the agent made that you have not looked at, and how it earns trust', shot='23-controlpanel-track-record.jpg',
    shot_alt='The trust section of the Control panel: the days setting for approvals not reviewed, the three radio choices for which reviewed cases count, and the box showing where the track record stands now for extensions and trial credit.',
    shot_caption='The section about approvals you have not looked at. The box at the bottom shows the live track record counts for each kind of request.',
    intro='This section is about the agent\'s score card. Every approval the agent makes is listed on the Decide page for you to agree with, disagree with or override. Here you choose what happens to approvals you never get round to reviewing, and which reviewed cases make up the score card.',
    items=[
        ('Count an approval the agent made as agreed when you have not reviewed it for (days)',
         'It only concerns approvals the agent has already made, inside your rules. It never makes the agent decide a request that is waiting for you. If you leave an approval alone for this many days, it is recorded as "Treated as agreed" and counts as agreement in the track record. It is labelled as such and you can still disagree later.',
         'Value 7. The agent approved Bayview\'s extension on Monday. You do nothing. The next Monday it shows "Treated as agreed after 7 days" and adds one agreement to the score card. Empty means an unreviewed approval stays "not reviewed" and counts neither for nor against the agent.',
         'Approvals you ignore count as agreement for longer. The score card grows more slowly.',
         'Silence turns into agreement sooner. Empty switches it off.',
         'Default: empty (off), so only reviews you actually give count. Suggestion: leave it empty in the first week. Range is 1 to 365 days. It is not applied while delegation is paused or to a decision you overrode.',
         'num'),
        ("Which reviewed cases count as the agent's track record",
         'The track record is the agent\'s score card: of the reviewed cases your rules would approve, how often you agreed. This choice says which cases are added up. The score card only holds the agent back when you set a minimum under Advanced ("Track record: reviewed cases needed before the agent decides" and "Track record: how often you must have agreed (%)"). Without a minimum it is only shown.',
         'In the demonstration the policy would approve 6 of the practice cases for extensions and the fictional Owner agreed with 5 of them. Fictional cases only counts those 6 practice cases. Live cases only counts your own reviews (0 at the start). Both, pooled adds them together.',
         'The three choices are "Fictional cases only", "Live cases only" and "Both, pooled (default)". Fictional means the made-up practice cases that come with the demonstration data. They are a simulation, not real customers. The answers you give in the Model page questionnaire "Teach it your limits" do not count here. They feed similar cases and the model\'s training table.',
         'Choosing "Live cases only" makes the agent earn trust on real decisions alone. With a minimum of 5, requests of that kind come to you until you have reviewed 5.',
         'Default: "Both, pooled". Suggestion: for a real business, Live cases only once you have a few weeks of reviews. For the class demonstration, leave it on the default.',
         'pick'),
    ]))

GROUPS.append(dict(
    id='cp-exceptions', title='Exceptions the agent may decide', shot='24-controlpanel-exceptions.jpg',
    shot_alt='The Exceptions section of the Control panel: a switch, five number fields for the size of the exception and the two safeguards, and the field for preparing a decline.',
    shot_caption='The Exceptions section. Everything here is off by default.',
    intro='Normally a request over a limit comes to you. This section lets the agent approve one that is a little over a money limit, or a few days over the longest extension, but only when both safeguards hold: the team\'s model says you would probably approve it, and you approved enough of the most similar earlier cases. Any other broken rule still sends the request to you. The model must be fitted first (Model page). With no model fitted, no exception is ever approved.',
    items=[
        ('The agent may approve these exceptions',
         'The master switch for this section. Off by default. Off means a request over a limit always comes to you.',
         'Switch on with a limit of 450 and Bayview\'s extension of 480 (6.7% over). If the model says you would approve it with enough confidence and you approved enough similar cases, the agent approves and says "Approved as an exception inside the band you delegated". Otherwise it comes to you with the reason it did not apply.',
         'Turn it on to allow exceptions. It also makes "Prepare a decline for you" below work.',
         'Turn it off and every request over a limit comes to you.',
         'Default: off. Suggestion: leave it off until you have decided about 20 requests yourself, so the model has something to learn from.',
         'flag'),
        ('How far over a money limit the agent may go (% over the limit)',
         'A percentage of the limit, so the room grows with the limit. A request further over than this always comes to you.',
         'Limit 1,000. A value of 5 allows up to 1,050. A value of 50 allows up to 1,500. With a limit of 5,000,000, 5 allows 250,000 over.',
         'The agent may go further over a limit.',
         'The agent stays closer to the limit. 0 allows no money exception.',
         'Default: 50. Suggestion: start with 5 or 10 and raise it only if the exceptions the agent made were ones you would have approved.',
         'num'),
        ('How many days over the longest extension the agent may go',
         'The same idea for time. It is the number of extra days past "Longest single extension".',
         'Longest extension 30 and this set to 15: the agent may approve up to 45 days. A request for 46 days comes to you.',
         'The agent may approve longer extensions as exceptions.',
         'The agent stays closer to the longest extension. 0 allows no day exception.',
         'Default: 15. Suggestion: 5 or less in the first weeks.',
         'num'),
        ('Safeguard 1: the chance of your approval the model must give (%)',
         'The model estimates, from the decisions you made before, the chance that you would approve this request. This is the lowest estimate the agent will accept before making an exception. The model works with the chance that you would decline. An approval chance of 80% is a decline chance of 20%.',
         'Value 80. In a test on the demonstration data the model put the chance that you approve Bayview\'s extension at 72%, under the 80% required, so the request came to the Owner and said so. Lowering this bar to 50 and the second safeguard to 1 let the same request through.',
         'Higher is stricter: fewer exceptions are made.',
         'Lower is looser: more exceptions are made, on weaker evidence.',
         'Default: 80. Suggestion: keep 80 or higher.',
         'num'),
        ('Safeguard 2: of the 5 most similar earlier cases, at least this many you approved',
         'Nucleon finds the 5 earlier cases most like this one (same kind, similar amount and days). You must have approved at least this many of them. It is a second opinion that does not depend on the model.',
         'Value 3. Of the 5 closest cases you approved 4: the safeguard holds. If you approved only 2, the request comes to you.',
         'A higher number is stricter. 5 means all five must have been approved.',
         'A lower number is looser. 1 means one approved case is enough.',
         'Default: 3. Suggestion: 4 for a cautious start.',
         'num'),
        ("Prepare a decline for you when the model's chance of a decline is at least (%)",
         'This does not decline anything. When a case comes to you and the model thinks you would probably decline, Nucleon writes the reason and gives you one click on the Decide page. You still decide. It works only while "The agent may approve these exceptions" is on. Empty means never prepare one.',
         'Value 80. The model puts the chance of a decline at 85% on Harbourline\'s request. The Decide page then shows a prepared decline with the reason written. You press one button, or decide something else.',
         'Lower it: more prepared declines appear.',
         'Raise it, or empty it: fewer or none.',
         'Default: 80. Suggestion: keep the default. Nothing happens without your click.',
         'num'),
    ]))

